# ---------------------------------------------------------------- ABSTRACT
OPS[8] = ('REPLACE', ["""Sexual violence against disabled people is well documented epidemiologically and forcefully denounced politically, yet it has rarely been theorized as a product of the ordinary organization of care. This conceptual article develops such a theorization from the medico-social systems of Belgium and France, where residential and semi-residential facilities remain the primary infrastructure of support for many disabled people and where independent safeguarding mechanisms are comparatively weak. Drawing on crip theory (McRuer, 2006; Kafer, 2013), feminist disability studies (Garland-Thomson, 2011; Wendell, 1996), epistemic injustice frameworks (Fricker, 2007; Williams & Jobe, 2024), and disability justice (Berne et al., 2018; Mingus, 2017), we argue that forced intimacy is not one risk factor among others but the structural medium through which normative and epistemic systems are joined. Because intimate bodily access is mandated and routinized as care, it produces exposure to violation and, in the same movement, disables its recognition: epistemic injustice operates before disclosure, foreclosing the perceptual and interpretive resources through which violation could be named, and not only after it, in the discounting of testimony already given. We trace how disclosures are subsequently absorbed by institutions through the reattribution of signs to impairment, the pre-emptive disqualification of the disabled person's account, and the diffusion of reporting responsibility. We then argue that the analysis is portable beyond the regime in which we develop it, to other configurations in which relational or bodily access is professionally mandated and interpretive authority is professionally held. Implications for feminist social work practice, education, and research are discussed."""])

OPS[10] = ('REPLACE', ["""Keywords: crip theory, forced intimacy, epistemic injustice, sexual violence, disability, feminist social work"""])

