OPS[15] = ('REPLACE', ["""This disconnection is not incidental. Crip theory has extensively theorized normative corporeality, institutional power, and the politics of care and sexuality (McRuer, 2006; Kafer, 2013; Price, 2015), but has largely left sexual violence as a named reality rather than a theorized object. Feminist social work has produced rich frameworks for understanding gender-based violence, institutional complicity, and survivor-centered practice, but has rarely treated disability as a constitutive rather than supplementary dimension of analysis. The consequences are concrete: disabled survivors encounter services and practitioners ill-equipped to recognize the specific conditions of their exposure, and researchers lack the conceptual tools to analyze those conditions without defaulting to individualistic framings of vulnerability."""])

OPS[18] = ('REPLACE', ["""The concept of vulnerability is where this disconnection is most visible and most consequential. When disability and sexual violence are addressed together, the dominant framing positions disabled people as inherently vulnerable: their bodies or minds are understood to create a special susceptibility that calls for protection. This framing organizes the epidemiological literature, in which impairment type functions as the primary explanatory variable (Hughes et al., 2012; Mailhot Amborski et al., 2022); it organizes public policy, as in the French Senate report on violence against disabled women, whose very framing of the problem is the denunciation of an invisible vulnerability (Courteau et al., 2019); and it has been documented as the operative common sense of practitioners and services in the studies that set out to contest it (Hollomotz, 2009, 2011; Thiara et al., 2012). It has been extensively criticized from within disability studies (Hollomotz, 2009, 2011) and feminist theory (Fineman, 2008; Wendell, 1996), and a substantial body of critical disability and critical social work scholarship has developed alternative analyses of structural violence, institutional harm, and the carceral organization of care (Ben-Moshe, 2020; Chapman & Withers, 2019; Rossiter & Rinaldi, 2018; Shaikh et al., 2022; Steele, 2020). That a structural account is needed is therefore not our claim, and would not be news to critical or feminist social work. Our claim concerns the mechanism: how, specifically, the arrangements through which care is delivered produce both the exposure and its unsayability, and what follows for the professions that staff those arrangements."""])

OPS[22] = ('REPLACE', ["""We argue that sexual violence against disabled people in these settings is best understood not as an expression of individual fragility but as an effect of three interlocking systems. The first is normative: McRuer's (2006) compulsory able-bodiedness produces a hierarchy of bodies in which disabled corporealities are rendered available for forms of control and intrusion that non-disabled bodies are not, and Mingus's (2017) concept of forced intimacy names the everyday dimension of this normative arrangement in care relationships. The second is epistemic: disabled people who report sexual violence face a systematic credibility deficit (Williams & Jobe, 2024) that Fricker's (2007) framework of testimonial injustice allows us to name precisely, compounded by what Freyd and colleagues theorize as institutional betrayal (Smith & Freyd, 2013, 2014) and by what Samra (2025) reframes as a deeper, ontological injustice that operates before disclosure. The third is professional: the caring occupations that staff these institutions, feminist social work among them, work through relational forms that carry psycho-emotional disablism (Reeve, 2002, 2012) and curative logics (Graziano, 2026), and that can therefore reproduce the asymmetries of power in which violence thrives even where the professional commitment is to contest them."""])

OPS[24] = ('REPLACE', ["""What is new here is not any of these concepts taken singly but the claim their articulation makes possible. In the continental medico-social regime, forced intimacy is not one risk factor among others; it is the structural medium that binds the normative system to the epistemic one. Because intimate bodily access is mandated and routinized as care, it produces exposure to violation and, in the same movement, disables its recognition. Epistemic injustice therefore operates not only after a disclosure, in the discounting of a disabled person's testimony, but before it, by foreclosing the perceptual and interpretive resources through which violation could be named at all. Each field, taken alone, has missed this pre-disclosure conjunction: crip theory has theorized compulsory able-bodiedness without following it into the scene of sexual violence; feminist social work has theorized gender-based violence without treating disability as a constitutive axis; and the epistemic injustice literature has largely addressed credibility once testimony has been given.""",
"""Two consequences organize the article. The first is analytical: if exposure and unsayability are produced together by the same arrangement, then interventions that address only the second — better disclosure practice, more attentive listening, improved credibility — leave the first intact. The second is a matter of scope. The mechanism we describe is densest in the continental medico-social regime, which is why we specify it there, but nothing in it is proper to that regime or to disability. It should be expected wherever bodily or relational access to a person is professionally mandated and the authority to interpret that access is professionally held. We return to this portability, and to its limits, in the discussion."""])

OPS[26] = ('REPLACE', ["""The article is structured as follows. The next section clarifies the conceptual approach, positionality, and scope of the argument, including the occupational vocabulary at stake. A background section then situates the contribution within critical disability studies and critical social work scholarship on institutional violence, and within the francophone theorization of the medico-social. Three substantive sections take up, in turn, the normative infrastructure of compulsory able-bodiedness and forced intimacy, which shifts the analytical frame from individual vulnerability to structural exposure; the epistemic and institutional mechanisms — testimonial, hermeneutical, and ontological injustice, and institutional betrayal — through which this violence is rendered invisible; and the professional register, where the analysis bears most directly on feminist social work. The discussion establishes the scope of the argument beyond the medico-social regime and draws out implications for practice, education, and research."""])

# -------------------------------------------- CONCEPTUAL APPROACH / SCOPE
OPS[31] = ('REPLACE', ["""This is a conceptual article. It does not present empirical findings, and it does not claim to provide an exhaustive review of the literature on disability and sexual violence. The framework it develops is not derived from the literature alone, however: it emerges from, and is shaped to make sense of, our own ongoing empirical research on how French and Belgian medico-social institutions and the professionals working in them recognize, deny, or absorb disclosures of sexual violence by disabled people (Authors, under review). It is worth being precise about what that fieldwork contributed, since the empirical paper is under review and its data are not available to readers here. Three things in the argument below are field-derived rather than literature-derived. The first is the salience of forced intimacy itself: what recurred in interviews and field journals was not a catalogue of risk factors but the sheer ordinariness of mandated bodily access, and the difficulty professionals and residents alike had in drawing a line inside it. The second is the observation that disclosures were rarely refused outright and were instead metabolized — reattributed, requalified, deferred — which is what directed us toward institutional betrayal and away from a model of individual disbelief. The third is the sequencing: the interpretive foreclosure we describe appeared to precede rather than follow the moment of disclosure. These are the questions the fieldwork put to us. The answers we give are conceptual, and every claim advanced in this article is warranted by published scholarship and publicly available institutional sources, which we cite as such; readers are not asked to take unpublished data on trust.""",
"""The corpus we engage was assembled deliberately, through theoretical triangulation across four bodies of scholarship — crip theory, feminist disability studies, epistemic injustice frameworks, and feminist and critical social work — that we read as offering complementary resources for an integrated account. We engage seminal and recent works in each of these traditions, alongside disability justice scholarship that does not always appear in academic databases but constitutes a foundational contribution to the theorization of disability and violence (Berne et al., 2018; Mingus, 2011, 2017; Piepzna-Samarasinha, 2018; Sins Invalid, 2018). We engage activist and non-academic scholarship as a normal feature of disability studies inquiry, not as a methodological exception requiring justification."""])

OPS[34] = ('REPLACE', ["""Our analytical strategy follows the tradition of intersectional theory-building (Collins & Bilge, 2020; Crenshaw, 1991): we treat the normative, epistemic, and professional systems analyzed here not as parallel or additive structures but as mutually constitutive. Compulsory able-bodiedness underwrites epistemic hierarchies that in turn shape professional practice, while professional practice reproduces the normative arrangements from which it derives its authority. What we claim is therefore neither the discovery of any single one of these dynamics, each theorized within its own tradition, nor a general call for structural analysis, which critical and feminist social work has been issuing for decades. We claim an account of how the three lock together in a specific institutional regime — with forced intimacy as the hinge — and of what that account makes visible that a structural framing alone does not: that the conditions of exposure and the conditions of its unsayability are the same conditions."""])

