OPS[42] = ('REPLACE', ["""A note on scope and vocabulary is also warranted. By “disability” we follow the convention of contemporary disability studies and disability justice movements, treating the term as a social and political position rather than a list of impairments. Where our argument applies with particular force to specific groups — people with intellectual disabilities, people with psychosocial or psychiatric disabilities, people with neurodevelopmental disabilities — we name them explicitly, recognizing that the credibility hierarchies, communication conditions, and institutional arrangements at issue affect these groups in distinct and compounded ways (Daley et al., 2019; Samra, 2025; Williams & Jobe, 2024). We use “crip” as a reclaimed critical term following McRuer (2006) and Kafer (2013), not as a neutral descriptor.""",
"""The occupational vocabulary requires more care than it usually receives, because the argument turns on a division of labour that Anglophone usage tends to collapse. Contrary to a common assumption, France and Belgium do have a regulated social work profession: the *assistant de service social*, certified in France by a state diploma (the *diplôme d'État d'assistant de service social*), whose work is oriented toward rights, entitlements, coordination, and case management. Intimate bodily care in residential settings is not normally performed by this professional. It is performed by *aides-soignant·es*, *auxiliaires de vie*, and — for a broader educational and daily-living mandate — *éducateur·rices spécialisé·es*, occupations with their own training routes, their own status hierarchies, and, in the first two cases, markedly lower pay and job security. When we speak of forced intimacy we are describing the work of these frontline occupations. When we speak of the assessment, requalification, and closure of a disclosure we are usually describing the work of a *référent* or case manager, a position that in these settings is held by an *assistant de service social* or an *éducateur·rice spécialisé·e*. The distinction matters analytically, and we mark it throughout: the social worker's characteristic position in the configuration we describe is not that of the person whose hands are on the body, but that of the person who decides what the hands meant. That is a position of considerable epistemic power, and it is the one this article addresses. By “feminist social work” we therefore refer both to the Anglophone tradition of feminist and anti-oppressive social work theory (Dominelli, 2002; Fook, 2002) and to the gendered care professions of continental Europe — *travail social*, *aide sociale*, *éducation spécialisée* — which are not identical to professional social work in the Anglophone sense but occupy structurally analogous positions in the medico-social organization of disabled life."""])

