OPS[44] = ('REPLACE', ["""The account we develop builds on, rather than fills a void in, two long-standing critical traditions. Both critical disability studies and critical social work have theorized institutional violence, the carceral organization of care, and the imbrication of disablism with other axes of oppression for decades. Naming this prior work is essential, because it sets the standard the present contribution has to meet: not to introduce structural analysis into fields that already have it, but to specify a mechanism these traditions have described in its effects without isolating in its operation, and to do so on institutional ground they were not built from."""])

# merge 045 + 046 into two tighter paragraphs
OPS[45] = ('REPLACE', ["""Critical disability studies and the analysis of institutional violence. The critique of institutional violence against disabled people is constitutive of contemporary disability studies rather than an emergent agenda. Sobsey's (1994) early synthesis already documented institutional and semi-institutional settings as primary sites of abuse, and a substantial scholarship has since developed the analysis well beyond the question of individual perpetrators. Ben-Moshe's (2020) *Decarcerating Disability* and the collection she co-edited on *Disability Incarcerated* (Ben-Moshe et al., 2014) reconceptualize disability institutions as continuous with the carceral state, refusing the analytic separation between prison, psychiatric hospital, group home, and residential facility; this work is written from disability studies and critical criminology rather than from social work, and its purchase on social work is one of implication rather than of disciplinary membership. Rossiter and Rinaldi's (2018) Canadian case studies of long-term care demonstrate how violence is produced through ordinary institutional functioning rather than aberrant practice, and Steele (2020) theorizes the legal infrastructures through which institutional harm is rendered lawful. In Mad Studies, Daley, Costa, and Beresford's (2019) collection gathers a sustained critique of psychiatric institutional violence that intersects directly with the questions of credibility and coercion we raise. These literatures share a structural analytic: violence against disabled people is constitutive of institutional life rather than exceptional to it."""])

OPS[46] = ('REPLACE', ["""This critique has been deepened by sub-traditions that refuse the universalization of the disabled subject. Mad Studies (Daley et al., 2019; LeFrançois et al., 2013) has insisted that psychiatric power and the violence of involuntary intervention require their own analytic vocabulary. DisCrit (Annamma et al., 2013) has theorized the co-production of disability and race in carceral and educational systems, and Schalk's (2018, 2022) Black disability studies, Erevelles's (2011) materialist analysis, Kim's (2017) crip-of-color critique, and Bell's (2006) intervention on White disability studies have collectively reoriented the field away from single-axis politics. These are not optional additions to a generic disability analysis: they specify the mechanisms through which disabled people of color, disabled women of color, and disabled queer and trans people experience compounding exposure to violence that single-axis frameworks systematically miss."""])

OPS[47] = ('REPLACE', ["""Critical social work as a tradition of structural critique. Critical social work has engaged the same terrain from its own angle. Chapman and Withers' (2019) *A Violent History of Benevolence* analyzes social work as a profession constituted through interlocking moral economies of colonial, racial, ableist, and class-based violence, displacing the field's self-presentation as a profession of care; both authors write from within social work while also being read, and locating themselves, within disability studies, and the boundary between the two fields is in their case genuinely porous rather than merely crossed. Shaikh, LeFrançois, and Macías's (2022) *Critical Social Work Praxis* assembles a critical-theoretical apparatus for thinking about race, madness, gender, and the state in practice, while Dominelli's (2002) anti-oppressive and Fook's (2002) critical social work provide foundational vocabularies for power asymmetries in professional relationships. Within feminist social work specifically, Wendell's (1989, 1996) feminist disability theory and Morris's (1991) engagement with the able-bodied assumptions of feminist practice opened, decades ago, the internal critique we extend here."""])

OPS[48] = ('REPLACE', ["""A francophone theorization of the medico-social. The Anglophone literatures on which this article principally draws emerged from contexts in which deinstitutionalization, statutory rights frameworks, and survivor-led advocacy networks have substantial institutional purchase. The continental medico-social is a distinct regime of disability governance with its own genealogy. Stiker's (2013) *longue durée* history of the figure of infirmity in French culture, Castel's (1995) analysis of the social question and its *dispositifs*, and Revillard's (2020) sociology of disability rights as “vulnerable rights” together specify a tradition in which large residential and semi-residential institutions are not residual anachronisms but active components of public welfare; in which professional authority is distributed across medical, paramedical, and educational actors around a normative horizon of social inclusion that retains paternalist structures in practice; and in which disabled-led advocacy has historically been mediated by parent associations and public administrators rather than by independent disabled subjects (Ville et al., 2020).""",
"""It is from this inheritance that the francophone vocabulary of *validisme* and *capacitisme* emerges, and since both terms are sometimes presented as simple translations of “ableism” it is worth saying what they are. *Validisme*, the older and more widely used of the two, is built from *valide* — the everyday French term for a non-disabled person, and, revealingly, also the term for what is legally or administratively valid. It names a system that treats the non-disabled body as the standard of the fully human and organizes institutions accordingly, and it was forged inside activist critique of the French institutional apparatus, so that its primary referents are placement, medical gatekeeping, and administrative categorization rather than attitude or prejudice (Masson, 2013; Puiseux, 2022). *Capacitisme*, a more recent coinage modelled morphologically on *racisme* and *sexisme* and built on *capacité*, foregrounds instead the ranking of persons by measured capacity and travels more readily to contexts — schooling, employment, evaluation — where what is at stake is graded performance rather than institutional placement. The two are used interchangeably by many authors and distinguished by others; the distinction we draw on here is that *validisme* names a regime and *capacitisme* a metric. Neither is a translation of “ableism” in the sense that neither was produced by translating it: both were produced by politicizing a specific medico-social inheritance, which is why they carry institutional referents that the Anglophone term, formed in a different institutional history, does not."""])

OPS[49] = ('REPLACE', ["""What this article adds. Read together, these literatures establish that sexual violence against disabled people is not under-theorized for lack of critical resources. Critical disability studies has theorized institutional violence; Mad Studies has theorized psychiatric coercion; DisCrit and Black disability studies have theorized the racialized production of disablement; critical social work has theorized the violence of benevolent professions. Two things nonetheless remain undone. The first is analytical: none of these traditions has isolated the mechanism by which a single arrangement produces both exposure to sexual violation and the impossibility of naming it — which is what treating forced intimacy as a medium rather than a risk factor allows us to do. The second is geographical: the Anglophone scholarship we engage emerges from contexts shaped by deinstitutionalization, statutory safeguarding, and disabled-led advocacy infrastructures with no direct equivalents in Belgium and France, while the francophone scholarship on disability, gender, and violence (Bouchet, 2024; Bouchet et al., 2025a; Masson, 2013; Puiseux, 2022; Revillard, 2020) has begun to map this terrain without systematically engaging crip theory, disability justice, or epistemic injustice. This article works in the space defined by both."""])

# --------------------------------------- VULNERABILITY -> STRUCTURAL EXPOSURE
OPS[51] = ('REPLACE', ["""The dominant framing of disabled people's exposure to sexual violence relies on a concept of vulnerability understood as inherent, located in the disabled body or mind itself. In this framing, cognitive impairment reduces the capacity to recognize danger; physical dependence limits the capacity to resist or escape; communication difficulties prevent disclosure. The logic is additive: disability accumulates risk factors, and intervention is conceived as protective management of a pre-given fragility. The framing is not a straw figure. It structures the meta-analytic literature, where impairment category is the variable through which elevated prevalence is explained (Hughes et al., 2012; Mailhot Amborski et al., 2022); it structures francophone public policy, where the French Senate's report on violence against disabled women builds its case on the denunciation of an unseen vulnerability (Courteau et al., 2019); it was the operative assumption of the services studied by Hollomotz (2009, 2011) and by Thiara and colleagues (2012); and it persists in the safeguarding vocabularies through which continental medico-social institutions describe their own populations (Revillard, 2020). This framing is not simply theoretically inadequate. It is actively harmful. By locating vulnerability in the person rather than in the conditions surrounding them, it naturalizes the very arrangements that produce exposure, deflects attention from institutional responsibility, and reproduces the paternalistic logics that disability justice movements have identified as foundational to disablist oppression (Hollomotz, 2009; Mingus, 2017; Sins Invalid, 2018)."""])

OPS[55] = ('REPLACE', ["""The intersectional dimension is immediately visible once race and class enter the analysis. Schalk (2018, 2022) shows that the production of disability through racial violence — police brutality, incarceration, poverty, inadequate healthcare — means that disabled people of color navigate compounding exposure within institutional landscapes where the hierarchies of compulsory able-bodiedness are overlaid by racial regimes that further devalue their testimony and deepen their dependency on institutional actors whose accountability is structurally limited. Erevelles (2011) makes the related materialist argument that disability, race, and class produce forms of structural exposure that frameworks treating these axes as separate rather than mutually constitutive cannot capture."""])

OPS[57] = ('REPLACE', ["""Crip theory connects this reframing to a broader analysis of normative power. McRuer's (2002, 2006) concept of compulsory able-bodiedness names the mandatory character of corporeal normalcy: just as Rich's (1980) compulsory heterosexuality describes a system in which heterosexuality is not simply preferred but required, compulsory able-bodiedness describes a system in which the able body is the unmarked norm against which other bodies are measured, found lacking, and subjected to corrective or controlling intervention. Price's (2011, 2015) work on the bodymind, refusing the dualism that separates physical from mental experience, establishes that this normative violence operates on the bodymind as an integrated whole. McRuer and Mollow's (2012) collection on sex and disability shows further that the simultaneous desexualization and hyperexposure of disabled sexuality constitutes a specific form of sexual governance, supplying the cultural context in which sexual violation of disabled people is rendered routine or invisible."""])

OPS[59] = ('REPLACE', ["""Puar's (2017) concept of debility adds a further dimension. Where disability names a recognized identity category that can make claims on the state, debility names the slow bodily and social attrition produced by poverty, racism, and structural neglect — a condition distributed unevenly across populations, produced deliberately as well as incidentally, and falling outside the frameworks through which disability rights are claimed. Debilitated people are exposed to violence in ways that are particularly difficult to name within existing social work frameworks, precisely because they are not the recognized subjects those frameworks are built to serve."""])

# 061 -> four paragraphs
OPS[61] = ('REPLACE', ["""It is Mingus's (2017) concept of forced intimacy that most directly names the structural link between compulsory able-bodiedness and the conditions of sexual violence. Forced intimacy designates the systematic requirement, built into the daily lives of many disabled people, to surrender bodily privacy and intimate access to strangers and institutional actors in order to meet basic needs: to be bathed, dressed, transferred, fed, medicated. This is not incidental to disabled life as currently organized; it is a structural feature of institutional and semi-institutional care arrangements, particularly marked in the medico-social systems of continental Europe, where residential and day-care facilities remain the primary infrastructure of support for many disabled people (Revillard, 2020). Mingus is explicit that forced intimacy is not the same as sexual violence, and equally explicit that it creates the conditions in which sexual violation becomes possible, legible as routine rather than transgressive, and difficult to name or resist.""",
"""That last claim is the one this article takes up, because the literature has named the relation more often than it has specified it. If forced intimacy is not itself sexual violence, by what mechanisms does it produce the conditions of violation? Three operate together, and each has an empirical anchor in the existing scholarship even where it has not been theorized in these terms.""",
"""The first is the desensitization of bodily boundary. When intimate physical contact with non-related others is a routine and unrenounceable feature of daily life, the perceptual and somatic markers that ordinarily signal violation — the unfamiliar hand, the unsanctioned proximity, the gesture that exceeds the task — lose their differentiating force. What would register as transgressive in another life is integrated, by repetition and by necessity, into the texture of care. This is the dynamic Sobsey (1994) identified when he showed that the compliance and deference systematically instilled in disabled people in institutional settings disable precisely the self-protective responses that recognizing violation requires, and that Hollomotz (2011) documents in the accounts of adults with learning difficulties for whom the question of what may be refused had never been posed.""",
"""The second is interpretive blurring. The institutional and professional vocabularies that frame intimate contact as “care” supply ready descriptions for acts that fall outside it. A care worker who exceeds what hygiene or transfer requires can locate the action within the same lexical and gestural field as legitimate care, and so can the institutional culture around them; what becomes difficult is not the action but its renaming as violation. Thiara and colleagues (2012) document the consequence at the level of professional response, where disclosures by disabled women were consistently metabolized as questions about the adequacy of care arrangements rather than as reports of violence.""",
"""The third is the asymmetry of interpretive authority. In care relationships organized by forced intimacy, the worker holds substantial authority to define what is happening — what counts as accidental, as necessary, as proportionate — while the disabled person's interpretation is filtered through institutional doubts about cognitive, communicative, or emotional reliability. Benedet and Grant's (2007, 2014) analysis of sexual assault complaints by women with mental disabilities shows how thoroughly this asymmetry is embedded even in legal settings designed to adjudicate it, and Williams and Jobe (2024) show it operating in the routine professional judgments through which police and social services assess disabled complainants. The next section takes up this asymmetry in its own right.""",
"""The relation between forced intimacy and sexual violence is thus neither identity nor analogy. Forced intimacy is the structural medium in which the perceptual, lexical, and authority conditions for sexual violation are assembled at once. This is why the arrangement cannot be addressed by improving the response to disclosure alone: the same arrangement that produces the exposure has already shaped what can be disclosed, in what terms, and with what standing."""])

OPS[63] = ('REPLACE', ["""Two further resources sharpen the point that exposure is a property of arrangements rather than of bodies. Garland-Thomson's (2011) concept of misfitting names it directly: a misfit emerges from the encounter between a body and an environment, not from a body in isolation, so that the harm is located in the fit and not in the person. Fineman's (2008) vulnerable subject theory has been read as offering a comparable exit from individualistic framings, but Arsenault-Gallant (2025) shows that it still locates the work of repair in the resilience of the subject: where resilience is what institutions are mandated to foster, those whose impairments are not amenable to that horizon are returned to the position of the deficient individual, and the arrangements that produced their exposure recede from view. The reframing this section proposes has to be held against that risk."""])

OPS[65] = ('REPLACE', ["""The shift traced in this section — from vulnerability as individual attribute to exposure as structural condition — is not itself novel, and we do not present it as such. Anti-oppressive and structural social work has long argued that disability is relational rather than individual and that practice must engage the arrangements producing exposure (Chapman & Withers, 2019; Dominelli, 2002; Fook, 2002; Shaikh et al., 2022), and within feminist disability scholarship Wendell (1989, 1996) and Morris (1991) opened this terrain decades ago. What the analysis of forced intimacy adds is specificity about the arrangement itself: not that exposure is structural, but that in this regime the structure is a mandated intimacy whose routinization simultaneously produces the exposure and dismantles the resources for naming it. That second half of the mechanism is what the following section develops."""])

