OPS[79] = ('REPLACE', ["""The institutional arrangements that disabled people navigate share the defining features Freyd identifies as conditions for institutional betrayal: they are environments of dependency and trust, with strong incentives to protect their own reputation, that routinely position the accounts of disabled residents as less authoritative than the professional judgments of staff. In the continental medico-social context these dynamics take specific forms, shaped by the tradition of specialized residential institutions serving large populations and by the comparative weakness of independent advocacy and external oversight (Revillard, 2020; Ville et al., 2020).""",
"""Three mechanisms of absorption can be distinguished, each documented in the published literature and in publicly available inspection and audit material, and each recurring in our own fieldwork across French and Belgian reporting systems (Authors, under review). The first is reattribution to impairment: possible signs of violation are absorbed into the explanatory reservoir of the disability itself, a bruise reattributed to known self-injurious movements, a change in behaviour to a syndrome or a medication, so that observations which would prompt concern in a non-disabled person are neutralized before they circulate. Peña-Guzmán and Reynolds (2019) name the epistemic form of this move; the French Senate inquiry documents its institutional consequences (Courteau et al., 2019). The second is pre-emptive disqualification: the disabled person's own account is discounted by invoking cognitive or communicative limitation before its content is examined, the mechanism Williams and Jobe (2024) trace through routine professional assessments of competence and consistency. The third is the diffusion of responsibility: where concern does surface, each professional assumes a colleague will report, the matter is noted in a file rather than escalated, and no formal *signalement* follows — the organizational pattern Thiara and colleagues (2012) describe when social workers and care managers prioritized the continuation of care arrangements over protection from violence.""",
"""What these three produce jointly is the inversion that institutional betrayal names: the institution protects the continuity of the care relationship and its own standing before it protects the resident. That this pattern holds across two jurisdictions with different statutory reporting duties suggests it is a property of the configuration rather than of a particular legal regime."""])

OPS[81] = ('REPLACE', ["""Testimonial injustice, hermeneutical and intimate hermeneutical injustice, institutional betrayal, and epistemic overconfidence thus constitute an interlocking system of silencing that operates largely beneath the threshold of conscious intent, reproduced through ordinary professional practice and ordinary institutional self-protection. Its relation to the previous section should now be clear: forced intimacy supplies the conditions under which these epistemic mechanisms have purchase, and they in turn make the arrangement that produced the exposure difficult to see as an arrangement at all. Social workers occupy a position within this system that is neither neutral nor fixed. They are among the professionals most likely to encounter disabled people at risk or in the aftermath of violation, and among those most thoroughly embedded in the institutional arrangements that produce silencing. The following section examines what that position requires."""])

