OPS[82] = ('REPLACE', ["""Inside the Configuration: Feminist Social Work between Absorption and Refusal"""])

OPS[83] = ('REPLACE', ["""Having analyzed the normative and epistemic systems, we turn to the professional register, and to the place feminist social work occupies within it. The claim we make here is deliberately narrow, and it is not the claim that feminist social work is the source of the problem. The problem is a configuration of mandated care that predates any particular profession and that would persist if social work vanished from it tomorrow. But social work is not outside that configuration either. In the medico-social regime described above, the professional who assesses a disclosure, decides its qualification, and determines whether it is escalated is very often a social worker or a *éducateur·rice spécialisé·e*; the frameworks through which that assessment is made are, in feminist and anti-oppressive services, frameworks feminist social work built. The question is therefore what happens to those frameworks when they operate inside a configuration organized by forced intimacy, and the answer, we argue, is that some of them are absorbed by it. Naming which ones, and how, is the condition of their refusal."""])

OPS[84] = ('REPLACE', ["""Consider, by way of orientation, the trajectory of a disclosure that this article is meant to make analytically legible. A woman in her thirties with a moderate intellectual disability has lived for fourteen years in a Belgian residential service. Daily care includes assistance with showering, dressing, transfers, and menstrual management, provided on rotating shifts by a small team of *aides-soignant·es* and *auxiliaires de vie*. Over a period of months, the contact of one of them — a man, in a workforce that is overwhelmingly composed of women, a disproportion that shapes both how his conduct is read and how hers is — begins to exceed what those tasks require. The woman tells another resident, then an *éducateur* in her day programme, that he “touches her wrongly.” The service's protocol routes the disclosure to her *référent*, the social worker responsible for her file, who knows the care worker professionally and has, on prior occasions, attributed her statements about interpersonal events to confusion or to the volatility associated with her diagnosis. The *référent* re-reads the disclosure through that prior frame: as a possible misperception of necessary care, as a likely conflict between resident and worker requiring mediation, as a candidate for closer behavioural monitoring rather than for safeguarding investigation. The care worker remains in post during the assessment. The woman, asked to clarify her account in successive interviews structured by professional vocabularies she does not share, becomes less consistent and more distressed; this is recorded as further evidence of unreliability. The case is closed without further action.""",
"""This trajectory is composite rather than singular, and its features are documented across the literature on institutional sexual violence (Hollomotz, 2011; Sobsey, 1994; Thiara et al., 2012) and in publicly available inspection and audit reports from Belgian and French regulators (Courteau et al., 2019). It is not exceptional. It is the routine product of the three systems working in concert: the normative condition of forced intimacy that makes the care worker's excess legible as continuous with legitimate care; the epistemic system that distributes credibility toward professional judgment and away from the resident's account; the professional system that absorbs the disclosure into institutional self-management.""",
"""The occupational geography of that trajectory deserves to be stated plainly, because it determines where this article's argument bites. Four positions are involved and they are not interchangeable. The *aide-soignant* or *auxiliaire de vie* performs the intimate bodily work in which forced intimacy consists; this is the least protected and least remunerated position in the chain, and it is also the position most exposed to the accusation and least equipped to contest it. The *éducateur·rice spécialisé·e* holds the daily relational mandate and is frequently the first recipient of a disclosure. The *référent* or case manager — the position typically held by an *assistant de service social* — holds the power of qualification: what the disclosure is, whether it is one, what happens next. Medical authority sits above all three and can requalify any of their judgments as clinical matters. Social work's characteristic position in this configuration is thus not the site of the intimate contact but the site of its interpretation, which is precisely why an analysis of epistemic power is the form the critique has to take. It is not that social workers commit the violence described here. It is that the point at which the configuration converts a disclosure into a non-event is, institutionally, a social work decision — and that the professional resources brought to that decision are what feminist social work is in a position to change."""])

OPS[86] = ('REPLACE', ["""The feminist disability scholarship on which this section draws insists that the relationship between feminism and disability is transformative rather than additive (Garland-Thomson, 1997, 2005). Wendell (1989, 1996) argues that taking disabled experience seriously as a theoretical resource requires feminism to revise its own assumptions about autonomy, productivity, and the normative body, and Morris (1991) shows that the values of independence and self-determination feminist social work has championed are themselves organized around an able-bodied norm: when independence is understood as freedom from reliance on others, it devalues the interdependence that characterizes disabled life. Thomas's (1999) concept of disablism — the social imposition of restrictions on disabled people's activity together with the undermining of their psycho-emotional wellbeing — provides the bridge from structural analysis to relational practice."""])

OPS[88] = ('REPLACE', ["""Reeve's (2002, 2012, 2014) concept of psycho-emotional disablism names that relational dimension with precision. Drawing on Foucault's analysis of normalization and Honneth's theory of recognition, Reeve argues that disablist oppression operates through interpersonal interaction and internalized self-perception: the experience of being spoken over, treated as less than fully competent, having one's judgments subordinated to those of professionals. Social workers, despite professional commitments to empowerment and anti-oppressive practice, routinely enact this through the ordinary conduct of professional relationships — communication styles that address carers rather than disabled people, assessment frameworks that center functional deficits rather than capacities and preferences — which is why Reeve (2012) argues that recognition should be taught as a foundational relational competency. In the context of sexual violence the stakes of that competency are severe: a disabled person whose account of violation is met with doubt, or redirected toward care needs, encounters in the social work relationship a replication of the epistemic silencing analyzed above. This is the mechanism by which the *référent*'s decision in our composite becomes possible without anyone in the chain intending harm."""])

OPS[90] = ('REPLACE', ["""Professional training compounds this. Where social workers and care staff are formed within frameworks that understand intimate physical contact as a legitimate and necessary feature of care work, they are structurally ill-equipped to recognize the same dynamics as conditions of violation when they arise inside care relationships. The social workers in Thiara and colleagues' (2012) study were not indifferent to the wellbeing of the disabled women they supported; the organizational frameworks within which they worked consistently prioritized maintaining care arrangements over protection from violence. The problem was not individual callousness but structural capture."""])

OPS[92] = ('REPLACE', ["""Curative logic is a second mechanism of absorption. Graziano (2026), drawing on McRuer's (2006) analysis of compulsory able-bodiedness, shows that caring professions are organized around an implicit telos of restoration to normalcy that positions the disabled person as a problem to be solved rather than as a person whose life as lived has value and whose bodily self-determination deserves respect. Kafer's (2013) analysis of curative time applies directly: when the goal of intervention is to move the disabled person toward approximating non-disabled norms, their own assessment of their situation — including their assessment of risk, violation, and harm — is subordinated to professional judgments about what a person at their level of functioning can reliably perceive and report."""])

OPS[94] = ('REPLACE', ["""Neither mechanism can be addressed through a disability-only lens. Schalk's (2022) analysis of Black disability politics shows that the organizations most effective at challenging the conditions producing disabled people's exposure to violence were those that treated racial and disability justice as inseparable, and Kim's (2017) crip-of-color critique insists that disability justice is most powerful as a methodology rather than an identity: a way of analyzing the production of exposure across multiple axes of power at once. That methodological orientation is what practice adequate to the intersectional realities of the disabled people social work encounters actually requires."""])

OPS[98] = ('REPLACE', ["""The ten principles of disability justice articulated by the Sins Invalid collective (Berne et al., 2018) provide the most developed framework for translating this orientation into practice, and three are particularly salient here. Intersectionality insists that disability cannot be addressed in isolation from race, gender, and class. Collective access challenges the individualized accommodation model that structures most disability-related intervention — a model that reproduces the isolation and dependency in which violation becomes possible — in favour of arrangements that build shared access into the design of services. Cross-movement solidarity insists that the struggle against sexual violence and the struggle against disablist oppression are interconnected rather than parallel, and that their separation weakens both."""])

OPS[100] = ('REPLACE', ["""Mingus's (2011) concept of access intimacy offers the most generative resource for what a crip feminist social work relationship might involve, because it is the structural inverse of the arrangement analyzed in this article. Where forced intimacy names compelled bodily access on institutional terms, access intimacy names the quality of a relationship or environment in which a disabled person's access needs are understood, anticipated, and met without requiring them to expose, explain, or justify themselves (Price, 2024). Applied to sexual violence response, it describes conditions in which a disabled person's knowledge of their own experience is received as authoritative rather than assessed for reliability — which is precisely the interpretive move our composite *référent* did not make. Piepzna-Samarasinha's (2018) concept of care webs extends the point to the organization of care itself, offering an image of support built on interdependence rather than professional authority, and a measure of the distance between that image and current practice."""])

# ---------------------------------------------------------------- DISCUSSION
OPS[101] = ('REPLACE', ["""Discussion: The Scope of the Argument, and What Follows for Practice"""])

OPS[102] = ('REPLACE', ["""We have argued that sexual violence against disabled people in the continental medico-social regime is produced by a configuration in which mandated intimate access, professionally held interpretive authority, and institutional self-protection operate together, and that this configuration produces the exposure and its unsayability in a single movement. Two questions follow. The first concerns scope: how far does the analysis reach beyond the regime in which we developed it? The second concerns practice: what does the analysis ask of feminist social work, and with what limits? We take them in that order, because the answer to the first constrains the answer to the second.""",
"""The scope of the argument: mandated care beyond the medico-social. We have specified the mechanism in Belgian and French medico-social institutions because that is where it is densest, best documented, and least theorized, and because the institutional particulars matter: the density of residential placement, the distribution of professional authority, the weakness of independent oversight. But the specification is not a boundary. What produces the conjunction we have described is not the continental medico-social regime as such; it is a set of conditions that regime instantiates with unusual clarity. Four are doing the work. Access to a person's body or life is institutionally mandated rather than chosen. That access is performed under a vocabulary of care, help, or treatment. The authority to interpret what happens within it is held professionally rather than by the person who undergoes it. And the institution that would receive a complaint is also the institution that arranges the access.""",
"""Wherever those four conditions hold, the same conjunction should be expected, and the same intervention — improving the reception of disclosures without altering the arrangement — should be expected to fail in the same way. This has direct implications for how the analysis travels. Deinstitutionalization in North America is an uneven and incomplete process rather than a completed transition, and the congregate settings that remain — group homes, nursing facilities, residential schools, forensic and psychiatric units — satisfy all four conditions (Ben-Moshe, 2020; Ben-Moshe et al., 2014; Rossiter & Rinaldi, 2018). More importantly, the conditions are not restricted to bodily intimacy or to disability. Where psychiatrized people live under community treatment orders, the mandated access is to compliance, to speech, and to the interior life: attendance is compulsory, disclosure to a worker is a condition of continued liberty, and non-compliance carries the threat of re-institutionalization, with the professional holding the authority to interpret what compliance and insight consist in (Daley et al., 2019; LeFrançois et al., 2013). In child protection, mandated access to the home and to family intimacy is exercised under a helping vocabulary, and the same authority interprets what is seen and said. In each case the intimacy at issue is relational or informational rather than physical, but the structure — compelled access, helping vocabulary, professionally held interpretation, complaint routed to the arranging institution — is the same, and so, we would predict, is the pre-disclosure foreclosure it produces.""",
"""We advance this as an analytic claim rather than an empirical finding: we have not studied those sites, and the forms violence takes in them are not identical to the forms we describe. What we would emphasize is that the analytic also specifies its own limits, which is what distinguishes it from a general appeal to structure. Where exit from the relationship is genuinely available, where independent advocacy is resourced and accessible, where the person's account holds standing that does not depend on professional ratification, or where the body receiving a complaint is not the body arranging the access, the conjunction weakens — and the mechanism predicts exactly that. This is why the institutional accountability measures discussed below are not peripheral to the argument. They are the points at which one of the four conditions can be removed."""])

OPS[104] = ('REPLACE', ["""The first implication concerns how vulnerability is taught. If exposure is produced by arrangements rather than by attributes, professional formation must equip practitioners to analyze arrangements rather than to manage the characteristics of at-risk individuals. Concretely, this means displacing risk assessment frameworks that treat impairment as the primary variable in favour of ecological analyses of care arrangements, institutional cultures, and normative environments (Hollomotz, 2009, 2011); introducing crip theory and disability justice not as specialized content for disability services practitioners but as analytical tools for all social workers; and building pedagogy that asks students to examine their own investments in curative logic — not through individualized reflection alone, but through structural analysis of the professional frameworks in which that logic is embedded (Dominelli, 2002; Reeve, 2012). The specific competence at issue is the one the composite *référent* lacked: the capacity to recognize that an interpretive frame built from prior professional judgments about a person is itself a mechanism of exposure."""])

OPS[106] = ('REPLACE', ["""The second implication concerns disclosure practice. Feminist social work has developed extensive knowledge about trauma-informed, survivor-centered practice in gender-based violence (Fook, 2002; Lonsway & Archambault, 2012), and the argument of this article is that such knowledge is necessary but insufficient here, because it addresses the reception of testimony and the mechanism we describe operates before testimony is given. Extending it requires three things. Practitioners need training to recognize and suspend the credibility hierarchies that position professional interpretation as more reliable than a disabled person's account, in the specific forms Williams and Jobe (2024) and Benedet and Grant (2007, 2014) document. Communication conditions need to be genuinely accessible rather than nominally accommodating, which in practice means that the interview format that made our composite's account less consistent is itself a safeguarding failure rather than an evidentiary finding. And disclosure has to be understood as a relational process shaped by the power dynamics of the professional relationship rather than as a discrete event to be assessed. Access intimacy is not a communication technique; it names a relationship in which a disabled person's epistemic authority over their own experience is affirmed before it is tested."""])

OPS[108] = ('REPLACE', ["""The third implication concerns institutional accountability, and it is here that the continental context is most specific and the leverage greatest, since these are the measures that remove conditions rather than mitigate consequences. In Belgium and France, where large residential institutions remain the primary infrastructure of support and independent advocacy is comparatively underdeveloped (Revillard, 2020; Ville et al., 2020), the decisive point is that the body receiving a complaint is usually the body arranging the care. Statutory instruments that could break that circuit already exist and are under-used rather than absent. In France, the *loi 2002-2* reforming social and medico-social action created a *charte des droits et libertés*, a *conseil de la vie sociale*, and a *personne qualifiée* whom users may call on to assert their rights; the *Défenseur des droits* offers an independent route for complaints of rights violations and discrimination; the *Contrôleur général des lieux de privation de liberté* monitors settings involving deprivation of liberty. Their limitation is uneven implementation, weak independence from provider organizations, and limited accessibility to residents with intellectual or communication disabilities.""",
"""Three developments would follow directly from our analysis. Reporting routes must terminate outside the provider organization, since a route that returns to the arranging institution reproduces the condition it is meant to remedy. Resident bodies need binding consultation rights over safeguarding policy rather than advisory status, so that the people who live inside the arrangement hold standing in its design. And monitoring capacity needs to sit with disabled-led organizations rather than with providers or their funders, on the model of the survivor advocacy that followed the Huronia litigation in Canada and of the UK self-advocacy networks that formed after Winterbourne View. We cite these as demonstrations that the absence of such mechanisms is contingent rather than necessary, not as templates for import: each was shaped by a specific legal and political context, and transposition would require attention to funding regimes and to the distribution of public and private provider authority. The point for feminist social work in Belgium and France is that institutional accountability is available as a concrete object of advocacy, research, and practice rather than as a horizon left unfilled by the Anglophone literature."""])

OPS[110] = ('REPLACE', ["""The fourth implication is for research. The analysis developed here depends on treating disability as a full axis of power analysis rather than a population characteristic — what Garland-Thomson (2002) calls integrating disability into feminist theory, not as an addendum to an already-constituted framework but as a transformation of it. Doing so exposes the able-bodied assumptions embedded in concepts that feminist social work treats as foundational: vulnerability, as this article has argued at length, but also autonomy, resilience, and empowerment, each of which presupposes a subject for whom exit, self-narration, and independent action are available. Collins and Bilge's (2020) intersectionality framework supplies the methodological tools for that work, on the condition that disability is treated as a full axis rather than appended to analyses of race, gender, and class."""])

OPS[112] = ('REPLACE', ["""This has a concrete research agenda attached. The literature we have engaged is almost entirely Anglophone and predominantly North American and British, while the francophone scholarship on disability, gender, and violence — growing rapidly, as Bouchet and colleagues (2025a, 2025b) and Revillard (2020) demonstrate — has not yet systematically engaged crip theory, disability justice, or epistemic injustice. Work in both directions is needed: the structural analysis proposed here should be developed in dialogue with the forms institutional betrayal takes in medico-social systems organized around different logics of disability governance, and the francophone theorization of *validisme* and *capacitisme* (Masson, 2013; Puiseux, 2022) should be engaged by Anglophone scholarship as a distinct politicization of a distinct institutional history rather than as a set of translations. The comparative question that follows from our scope claim is empirical and, as far as we are aware, open: whether the four conditions we have identified predict the absorption of disclosures across regimes that differ in their statutory architecture."""])

OPS[114] = ('REPLACE', ["""A final implication concerns the question, explicit within critical disability studies and critical social work, of whether the response to institutional violence should be reformist or abolitionist — and our analysis does not leave it neutral. Abolitionist positions, articulated most forcefully in the disability justice and Mad Studies traditions and developed by scholars working at the intersection of disability studies, criminology, and social work (Ben-Moshe, 2020; Ben-Moshe et al., 2014; Chapman & Withers, 2019), hold that institutions structured by mandated intimacy, dependency, and professional authority cannot be reformed in ways that durably remove the conditions of violation, and that the work is to dismantle large residential infrastructures in favour of community-based, disabled-led, interdependent support (Piepzna-Samarasinha, 2018; Sins Invalid, 2018). Reformist positions seek to make existing institutions less violent through training, safeguarding, advocacy, and survivor-centered practice. The four conditions we have identified give the abolitionist argument a specific form: three of them can in principle be addressed by reform, but the first — that access is mandated rather than chosen — is constitutive of congregate provision itself. We do not resolve the debate here. We note that the implications we have drawn for education, disclosure practice, and accountability are reformist in scope, necessary insofar as disabled people live in these settings now, and insufficient insofar as the abolitionist analysis of the first condition is correct; and that an analysis which locates the mechanism in a mandated arrangement cannot consistently treat the future of that arrangement as someone else's question."""])

OPS[115] = ('REPLACE', ["""To conclude, we return to what this article has argued. Sexual violence against disabled people in the continental medico-social regime is not a problem of individual vulnerability requiring protective management. It is produced by a configuration in which intimate access is mandated under a vocabulary of care, the authority to interpret that access is held professionally, and the institution that would hear a complaint is the institution that arranges the access. Forced intimacy is the medium binding that configuration together: it produces the exposure, and by routinizing intimate contact it dismantles the perceptual and interpretive resources through which violation could be named — which is why epistemic injustice here operates before disclosure and not only after it. Feminist social work sits inside this configuration at the point of interpretation, and its established resources for gender-based violence, built to improve the reception of testimony, do not by themselves reach a mechanism that operates upstream of testimony."""])

OPS[117] = ('REPLACE', ["""That is also the sense in which cripping feminist social work is not a specialization. It is the claim that disability is not one population among those the field serves but a constitutive axis of the arrangements through which it works, and that taking it as such changes what counts as an adequate response to violence: not only better listening, but the removal of the conditions that determined in advance what could be said."""])

