OPS[34] = ('REPLACE', ["""Our analytical strategy follows the tradition of intersectional theory-building (Collins & Bilge, 2020; Crenshaw, 1991): we treat the normative, epistemic, and professional systems analyzed here not as parallel or additive structures but as mutually constitutive. Compulsory able-bodiedness underwrites epistemic hierarchies that shape professional practice, while professional practice reproduces the normative arrangements from which it derives its authority. What we claim is an account of how the three lock together in a specific institutional regime, with forced intimacy as the hinge, and of what that account makes visible that a structural framing alone does not: that the conditions of exposure and the conditions of its unsayability are the same conditions."""])

OPS[41] = ('REPLACE', ["""A note on positionality is warranted. We write from a sociological and social work perspective situated in Belgium and France, engaging primarily Anglophone theoretical frameworks whose institutional and policy assumptions do not translate directly, since they were produced largely from contexts in which deinstitutionalization, independent living movements, and survivor-centered safeguarding have substantial institutional purchase. We name this asymmetry not to provincialize the analysis but to specify the institutional terrain on which our conceptual claims are meant to do work."""])

OPS[44] = ('REPLACE', ["""The account we develop builds on, rather than fills a void in, two long-standing critical traditions. Both critical disability studies and critical social work have theorized institutional violence, the carceral organization of care, and the imbrication of disablism with other axes of oppression for decades. Naming this work sets the standard the present contribution has to meet: to specify a mechanism these traditions have described in its effects without isolating in its operation, and to do so on institutional ground they were not built from."""])

OPS[53] = ('REPLACE', ["""The critical reframing begins with Hollomotz (2009, 2011), who argues that vulnerability must be understood ecologically rather than individually. Drawing on the social model of disability and on Kelly's (1988) continuum of sexual violence, she demonstrates that the conditions most consistently associated with elevated risk are not impairment characteristics but social arrangements: segregation from mainstream community life, overprotection that forecloses the development of self-protective knowledge, dependency on a small and closed network of caregivers, and the systematic absence of accessible information about rights, consent, and reporting. Her sociological extension of the argument (Hollomotz, 2013) places disablist violence on a continuum with the everyday subordination of disabled people's preferences and judgments to the authority of non-disabled others. Vulnerability, on this account, is a property of environments rather than of bodies."""])

OPS[65] = ('REPLACE', ["""The shift traced in this section is not itself novel, and we do not present it as such. What the analysis of forced intimacy adds is specificity about the arrangement: not that exposure is structural, but that in this regime the structure is a mandated intimacy whose routinization produces the exposure and dismantles the resources for naming it in the same movement. That second half of the mechanism is what the following section develops."""])

OPS[69] = ('REPLACE', ["""Fricker (2007) distinguishes two forms of epistemic injustice. Testimonial injustice occurs when a speaker's credibility is deflated by identity prejudice: the assessment of whether to believe what is said is distorted by stereotypes attached to the speaker's social position. Hermeneutical injustice operates more structurally, when the interpretive resources available in a given context are inadequate to make sense of a significant aspect of someone's experience — not because the person cannot describe what happened, but because the concepts and narrative frameworks that would let it be communicated and recognized are absent or distorted. Both operate with particular force when disabled people report sexual violence, and their interaction produces a compounded silencing further shaped by race, gender, and class. This asymmetry has an empirical anchor in francophone disability arenas: in mobilizations over disabled people's right to a sexual life, the authority to speak is routinely redistributed from disabled people, positioned as bearers of experiential testimony, to non-disabled professionals positioned as legitimate experts (Authors, 2018)."""])

OPS[71] = ('REPLACE', ["""The intersectional dimension of this silencing is not incidental. Kim's (2017) crip-of-color critique shows that the credibility hierarchies structuring disability-related epistemic injustice are compounded by racial stereotypes that further devalue the testimony of disabled people of color, and by gendered assumptions about emotional reliability and narrative coherence that bear with particular force on disabled women. Benedet and Grant (2007) document the compounding empirically: the legal system they examine divided disabled complainants into those understood as permanently incapable of consent and those understood as incapable of credible refusal, a binary that erased the contextual and relational character of their actual experiences. Their later analysis (Benedet & Grant, 2014) reaches a convergent conclusion: courts' inability to recognize the forms power and authority take in care relationships reflects not individual bias but a structural incapacity to interpret disabled women's experiences of violation."""])

OPS[73] = ('REPLACE', ["""Williams and Jobe (2024) document the testimonial dimension directly, in a qualitative study of people with learning disabilities and autism who reported sexual violence to police and social services in the United Kingdom. Their participants encountered a systematic assumption of unreliability: accounts filtered through stereotypes attributing confusion, suggestibility, and inconsistency to diagnostic categories, and assessed against implicit standards of the credible witness that the communication styles associated with neurodivergence or intellectual disability are unlikely to meet. Crucially, the credibility deficit operated through routine professional judgments about competence, consistency, and capacity that presented themselves as neutral assessments of evidence rather than as identity-based discounting."""])

OPS[75] = ('REPLACE', ["""The hermeneutical dimension is more insidious, because it operates before disclosure rather than in response to it — and it is here that the epistemic system meets the normative one. For disabled people living within care arrangements structured by forced intimacy, the resources for naming sexual violation are often systematically unavailable, because when intimate physical contact with carers is a routine feature of daily life the boundary between care and violation is blurred at the level of lived experience. Sobsey (1994) identified this early, showing that the compliance and deference instilled in institutional settings disable the self-protective responses that recognizing violation requires. Dohmen's (2016) concept of intimate hermeneutical injustice captures a further turn: the injustice is not only that others fail to understand the disabled person's experience, but that the person may lack access to the resources that would allow them to understand and communicate it themselves. Peña-Guzmán and Reynolds (2019) identify a related mechanism they term epistemic overconfidence: when a report of violation is reframed by a professional as behavioral disturbance or as a symptom of the person's condition, the disclosure is rendered unrecognizable as a disclosure at all."""])

OPS[77] = ('REPLACE', ["""Institutional betrayal (Smith & Freyd, 2013, 2014) adds a further dimension. The concept designates harm inflicted by an institution on individuals who depend on it and trust it to act in their interests — harm that consists not only in failing to prevent abuse but in creating conditions that make it more likely, normalizing the contexts in which it occurs, making reporting difficult or punitive, and responding inadequately when reports are made. Its relevance to disabled people in institutional and semi-institutional care is structural and immediate, yet it has been developed more fully within disability studies than within feminist social work scholarship on gender-based violence, where it remains comparatively underused."""])

OPS[96] = ('REPLACE', ["""Wright and Manuel's (2024) work on cripping and queering gender-based violence prevention is the most sustained recent attempt to translate these commitments into practice. They show that mainstream prevention frameworks reproduce the able-bodied norm by centering subjects assumed to have unimpaired access to information, resources, and exit options, rendering disabled survivors structurally invisible within systems designed without them. More generatively, they argue that disability justice offers a different foundation: interdependence rather than autonomy as the founding value, collective access rather than individual resilience as the goal, and the dismantling of forced intimacy and institutional dependency as primary prevention — which is to say, as an intervention on the conditions rather than on their consequences."""])

