OPS[8] = ('REPLACE', ["""Sexual violence against disabled people is well documented epidemiologically and forcefully denounced politically, yet it has rarely been theorized as a product of the ordinary organization of care. This conceptual article develops such a theorization from the medico-social systems of Belgium and France, where residential facilities remain the primary infrastructure of support for many disabled people and independent safeguarding is comparatively weak. Drawing on crip theory (McRuer, 2006; Kafer, 2013), feminist disability studies (Garland-Thomson, 2011; Wendell, 1996), epistemic injustice frameworks (Fricker, 2007; Williams & Jobe, 2024), and disability justice (Berne et al., 2018; Mingus, 2017), we argue that forced intimacy is not one risk factor among others but the structural medium through which normative and epistemic systems are joined. Because intimate bodily access is mandated and routinized as care, it produces exposure to violation and, in the same movement, disables its recognition: epistemic injustice operates before disclosure, foreclosing the resources through which violation could be named, and not only after it, in the discounting of testimony already given. We trace how disclosures are subsequently absorbed — through reattribution of signs to impairment, pre-emptive disqualification of the disabled person's account, and diffusion of reporting responsibility — and argue that the analysis is portable beyond this regime, to other configurations in which relational or bodily access is professionally mandated and interpretive authority professionally held. Implications for feminist social work practice, education, and research are discussed."""])

OPS[18] = ('REPLACE', ["""The concept of vulnerability is where this disconnection is most visible and most consequential. When disability and sexual violence are addressed together, the dominant framing positions disabled people as inherently vulnerable: their bodies or minds are understood to create a special susceptibility that calls for protection. This framing organizes the epidemiological literature, where impairment type functions as the primary explanatory variable (Hughes et al., 2012; Mailhot Amborski et al., 2022); it organizes public policy, as in the French Senate report on violence against disabled women, which builds its case on the denunciation of an invisible vulnerability (Courteau et al., 2019); and it has been documented as the operative common sense of services in the studies that set out to contest it (Hollomotz, 2009, 2011; Thiara et al., 2012). It has been criticized from within disability studies (Hollomotz, 2009, 2011) and feminist theory (Fineman, 2008; Wendell, 1996), and a substantial critical disability and critical social work scholarship has developed alternative analyses of structural violence, institutional harm, and the carceral organization of care (Ben-Moshe, 2020; Chapman & Withers, 2019; Rossiter & Rinaldi, 2018; Shaikh et al., 2022; Steele, 2020). That a structural account is needed is therefore not our claim, and would not be news to critical or feminist social work. Our claim concerns the mechanism: how, specifically, the arrangements through which care is delivered produce both the exposure and its unsayability."""])

OPS[19] = ('REPLACE', ["""We pursue that question from a continental European, and specifically Belgian and French, vantage. The medico-social systems through which Belgium and France organize support for disabled people remain heavily structured by large residential institutions, day-care facilities, and specialized services that operate outside the deinstitutionalization trajectories more familiar in Anglophone contexts (Revillard, 2020; Ville et al., 2020). Independent advocacy, statutory patient rights, external oversight, and survivor-led safeguarding, well established in parts of the United Kingdom, Canada, and the United States, remain comparatively underdeveloped (Courteau et al., 2019). The Anglophone scholarship we engage was not produced from this landscape, and its translation into Belgian and French social work is neither automatic nor neutral. Our aim is to bring crip theory, feminist disability studies, epistemic injustice frameworks, and disability justice into sustained dialogue with continental European scholarship that has begun to engage gender, disability, and violence (Bouchet, 2024; Bouchet et al., 2025a, 2025b; Masson, 2013; Puiseux, 2022) but has not yet articulated a structural account of sexual violence adequate to medico-social institutional life."""])

OPS[22] = ('REPLACE', ["""We argue that sexual violence against disabled people in these settings is best understood not as an expression of individual fragility but as an effect of three interlocking systems. The first is normative: McRuer's (2006) compulsory able-bodiedness produces a hierarchy of bodies in which disabled corporealities are rendered available for forms of control and intrusion that non-disabled bodies are not, and Mingus's (2017) forced intimacy names the everyday dimension of that arrangement in care relationships. The second is epistemic: disabled people who report sexual violence face a systematic credibility deficit (Williams & Jobe, 2024) that Fricker's (2007) testimonial injustice allows us to name precisely, compounded by institutional betrayal (Smith & Freyd, 2013, 2014) and by what Samra (2025) reframes as a deeper, ontological injustice operating before disclosure. The third is professional: the caring occupations that staff these institutions, feminist social work among them, work through relational forms that carry psycho-emotional disablism (Reeve, 2002, 2012) and curative logics (Graziano, 2026), and can therefore reproduce the asymmetries in which violence thrives even where the professional commitment is to contest them."""])

OPS[24] = ('REPLACE', ["""What is new here is not any of these concepts taken singly but the claim their articulation makes possible. In the continental medico-social regime, forced intimacy is not one risk factor among others; it is the structural medium binding the normative system to the epistemic one. Because intimate bodily access is mandated and routinized as care, it produces exposure to violation and, in the same movement, disables its recognition. Epistemic injustice therefore operates not only after a disclosure, in the discounting of testimony, but before it, by foreclosing the perceptual and interpretive resources through which violation could be named at all. Each field, taken alone, has missed this pre-disclosure conjunction: crip theory has theorized compulsory able-bodiedness without following it into the scene of sexual violence; feminist social work has theorized gender-based violence without treating disability as a constitutive axis; the epistemic injustice literature has largely addressed credibility once testimony has been given.""",
"""Two consequences organize the article. The first is analytical: if exposure and unsayability are produced together, interventions addressing only the second — better disclosure practice, more attentive listening — leave the first intact. The second concerns scope. The mechanism is densest in the continental medico-social regime, which is why we specify it there, but nothing in it is proper to that regime or to disability; it should be expected wherever bodily or relational access to a person is professionally mandated and the authority to interpret that access is professionally held. We return to this portability, and its limits, in the discussion."""])

OPS[31] = ('REPLACE', ["""This is a conceptual article. It does not present empirical findings, and it does not claim an exhaustive review of the literature on disability and sexual violence. The framework it develops is not derived from the literature alone, however: it emerges from our ongoing empirical research on how French and Belgian medico-social institutions and the professionals working in them recognize, deny, or absorb disclosures of sexual violence by disabled people (Authors, under review). Since that empirical paper is under review and its data are unavailable to readers here, it is worth being precise about what the fieldwork contributed. Three things in the argument are field-derived rather than literature-derived: the salience of forced intimacy itself, since what recurred was not a catalogue of risk factors but the sheer ordinariness of mandated bodily access; the observation that disclosures were rarely refused outright and were instead metabolized, which directed us toward institutional betrayal rather than individual disbelief; and the sequencing, since the interpretive foreclosure we describe appeared to precede rather than follow disclosure. These are the questions the fieldwork put to us. The answers we give are conceptual, and every claim advanced here is warranted by published scholarship and publicly available institutional sources, which we cite as such; readers are not asked to take unpublished data on trust.""",
"""The corpus was assembled through theoretical triangulation across four bodies of scholarship — crip theory, feminist disability studies, epistemic injustice frameworks, and feminist and critical social work — that we read as offering complementary resources for an integrated account. We engage seminal and recent works in each, alongside disability justice scholarship that does not always appear in academic databases but constitutes a foundational contribution to the theorization of disability and violence (Berne et al., 2018; Mingus, 2011, 2017; Piepzna-Samarasinha, 2018; Sins Invalid, 2018). We engage activist and non-academic scholarship as a normal feature of disability studies inquiry, not as a methodological exception requiring justification."""])

OPS[42] = ('REPLACE', ["""A note on scope and vocabulary is also warranted. By “disability” we follow the convention of contemporary disability studies and disability justice movements, treating the term as a social and political position rather than a list of impairments. Where our argument applies with particular force to specific groups — people with intellectual disabilities, people with psychosocial or psychiatric disabilities, people with neurodevelopmental disabilities — we name them explicitly, recognizing that the credibility hierarchies, communication conditions, and institutional arrangements at issue affect these groups in distinct and compounded ways (Daley et al., 2019; Samra, 2025; Williams & Jobe, 2024). We use “crip” as a reclaimed critical term following McRuer (2006) and Kafer (2013), not as a neutral descriptor.""",
"""The occupational vocabulary requires more care than it usually receives, because our argument turns on a division of labour that Anglophone usage tends to collapse. Contrary to a common assumption, France and Belgium do have a regulated social work profession: the *assistant de service social*, certified in France by a state diploma, whose work is oriented toward rights, entitlements, coordination, and case management. Intimate bodily care in residential settings is not normally performed by this professional. It is performed by *aides-soignant·es* and *auxiliaires de vie*, and, under a broader educational and daily-living mandate, by *éducateur·rices spécialisé·es* — occupations with their own training routes and status hierarchies and, in the first two cases, markedly lower pay and job security. When we speak of forced intimacy we describe the work of these frontline occupations; when we speak of the assessment and closure of a disclosure we usually describe the work of a *référent* or case manager, a position held by an *assistant de service social* or an *éducateur·rice spécialisé·e*. By “feminist social work” we refer both to the Anglophone tradition of feminist and anti-oppressive theory (Dominelli, 2002; Fook, 2002) and to the gendered care professions of continental Europe — *travail social*, *aide sociale*, *éducation spécialisée* — which are not identical to Anglophone professional social work but occupy structurally analogous positions in the medico-social organization of disabled life."""])

OPS[45] = ('REPLACE', ["""Critical disability studies and the analysis of institutional violence. The critique of institutional violence against disabled people is constitutive of contemporary disability studies rather than an emergent agenda. Sobsey's (1994) early synthesis already documented institutional and semi-institutional settings as primary sites of abuse, and scholarship has since developed the analysis well beyond the question of individual perpetrators. Ben-Moshe's (2020) *Decarcerating Disability* and the collection she co-edited on *Disability Incarcerated* (Ben-Moshe et al., 2014) reconceptualize disability institutions as continuous with the carceral state, refusing the analytic separation between prison, psychiatric hospital, group home, and residential facility; this work is written from disability studies and critical criminology rather than from social work, and its purchase on social work is one of implication rather than of disciplinary membership. Rossiter and Rinaldi (2018) show, through Canadian case studies of long-term care, that violence is produced through ordinary institutional functioning rather than aberrant practice; Steele (2020) theorizes the legal infrastructures through which such harm is rendered lawful; and in Mad Studies, Daley, Costa, and Beresford's (2019) collection gathers a critique of psychiatric institutional violence that bears directly on the questions of credibility and coercion we raise."""])

OPS[46] = ('REPLACE', ["""This critique has been deepened by sub-traditions that refuse the universalization of the disabled subject. Mad Studies (Daley et al., 2019; LeFrançois et al., 2013) insists that psychiatric power and involuntary intervention require their own analytic vocabulary; DisCrit (Annamma et al., 2013) theorizes the co-production of disability and race in carceral and educational systems; and Schalk's (2018, 2022) Black disability studies, Erevelles's (2011) materialist analysis, Kim's (2017) crip-of-color critique, and Bell's (2006) intervention on White disability studies have reoriented the field away from single-axis politics. These are not additions to a generic disability analysis: they specify how disabled people of color, disabled women of color, and disabled queer and trans people experience compounding exposure that single-axis frameworks systematically miss."""])

OPS[47] = ('REPLACE', ["""Critical social work as a tradition of structural critique. Chapman and Withers' (2019) *A Violent History of Benevolence* analyzes social work as a profession constituted through interlocking moral economies of colonial, racial, ableist, and class-based violence, displacing the field's self-presentation as a profession of care; both authors write from within social work while also locating themselves within disability studies, and the boundary is in their case genuinely porous rather than merely crossed. Shaikh, LeFrançois, and Macías's (2022) *Critical Social Work Praxis* assembles an apparatus for thinking about race, madness, gender, and the state in practice, while Dominelli (2002) and Fook (2002) provide foundational vocabularies for power asymmetries in professional relationships. Within feminist social work, Wendell (1989, 1996) and Morris (1991) opened, decades ago, the internal critique we extend here."""])

OPS[48] = ('REPLACE', ["""A francophone theorization of the medico-social. The continental medico-social is a distinct regime of disability governance with its own genealogy. Stiker's (2013) *longue durée* history of the figure of infirmity in French culture, Castel's (1995) analysis of the social question and its *dispositifs*, and Revillard's (2020) sociology of disability rights as “vulnerable rights” together specify a tradition in which large residential and semi-residential institutions are active components of public welfare rather than residual anachronisms; in which professional authority is distributed across medical, paramedical, and educational actors around a normative horizon of inclusion that retains paternalist structures in practice; and in which disabled-led advocacy has historically been mediated by parent associations and public administrators rather than by independent disabled subjects (Ville et al., 2020).""",
"""It is from this inheritance that the francophone vocabulary of *validisme* and *capacitisme* emerges, and since both are sometimes presented as simple translations of “ableism” it is worth saying what they are. *Validisme* is built from *valide*, the everyday French term for a non-disabled person and, revealingly, also for what is legally or administratively valid. It names a system that treats the non-disabled body as the standard of the fully human and organizes institutions accordingly; forged inside activist critique of the French institutional apparatus, its primary referents are placement, medical gatekeeping, and administrative categorization rather than attitude or prejudice (Masson, 2013; Puiseux, 2022). *Capacitisme*, a more recent coinage modelled on *racisme* and *sexisme* and built on *capacité*, foregrounds the ranking of persons by measured capacity and travels more readily to schooling, employment, and evaluation, where what is at stake is graded performance rather than institutional placement. Many authors use them interchangeably; the distinction we draw on is that *validisme* names a regime and *capacitisme* a metric. Neither is a translation of “ableism,” in the sense that neither was produced by translating it: both were produced by politicizing a specific medico-social inheritance, which is why they carry institutional referents the Anglophone term does not."""])

OPS[49] = ('REPLACE', ["""What this article adds. Read together, these literatures establish that sexual violence against disabled people is not under-theorized for lack of critical resources. Two things nonetheless remain undone. The first is analytical: none of these traditions has isolated the mechanism by which a single arrangement produces both exposure to sexual violation and the impossibility of naming it, which is what treating forced intimacy as a medium rather than a risk factor allows us to do. The second is geographical: the Anglophone scholarship we engage emerges from contexts shaped by deinstitutionalization, statutory safeguarding, and disabled-led advocacy with no direct equivalents in Belgium and France, while francophone scholarship on disability, gender, and violence (Bouchet, 2024; Bouchet et al., 2025a; Masson, 2013; Puiseux, 2022; Revillard, 2020) has begun to map this terrain without systematically engaging crip theory, disability justice, or epistemic injustice. This article works in the space defined by both."""])

OPS[51] = ('REPLACE', ["""The dominant framing of disabled people's exposure to sexual violence relies on a concept of vulnerability understood as inherent, located in the disabled body or mind itself. In this framing, cognitive impairment reduces the capacity to recognize danger; physical dependence limits the capacity to resist; communication difficulties prevent disclosure. The logic is additive: disability accumulates risk factors, and intervention becomes protective management of a pre-given fragility. The framing is not a straw figure. It structures the meta-analytic literature, where impairment category is the variable through which elevated prevalence is explained (Hughes et al., 2012; Mailhot Amborski et al., 2022); it structures francophone public policy (Courteau et al., 2019); it was the operative assumption of the services studied by Hollomotz (2009, 2011) and by Thiara and colleagues (2012); and it persists in the safeguarding vocabularies through which continental medico-social institutions describe their own populations (Revillard, 2020). The framing is not simply inadequate but actively harmful: by locating vulnerability in the person rather than in the conditions surrounding them, it naturalizes the arrangements that produce exposure, deflects attention from institutional responsibility, and reproduces the paternalistic logics that disability justice movements identify as foundational to disablist oppression (Hollomotz, 2009; Mingus, 2017; Sins Invalid, 2018)."""])

OPS[75] = ('REPLACE', ["""The hermeneutical dimension is more insidious, because it operates before disclosure rather than in response to it — and it is here that the epistemic system meets the normative one. For disabled people living within care arrangements structured by forced intimacy, the resources for naming sexual violation are often unavailable, because when intimate physical contact with carers is routine the boundary between care and violation is blurred at the level of lived experience. Sobsey (1994) identified this early, showing that the compliance and deference instilled in institutional settings disable the self-protective responses that recognizing violation requires. Dohmen's (2016) intimate hermeneutical injustice captures a further turn: the injustice is not only that others fail to understand the disabled person's experience, but that the person may lack access to the resources for understanding and communicating it themselves. Peña-Guzmán and Reynolds (2019) name a related mechanism, epistemic overconfidence: when a report of violation is reframed by a professional as behavioral disturbance or as a symptom of the person's condition, the disclosure is rendered unrecognizable as a disclosure at all."""])

OPS[83] = ('REPLACE', ["""Having analyzed the normative and epistemic systems, we turn to the professional register, and to the place feminist social work occupies within it. The claim here is deliberately narrow, and it is not that feminist social work is the source of the problem. The problem is a configuration of mandated care that predates any profession and would persist if social work vanished from it tomorrow. But social work is not outside that configuration either: in the regime described above, the professional who assesses a disclosure, decides its qualification, and determines whether it is escalated is very often a social worker or an *éducateur·rice spécialisé·e*, and the frameworks through which that assessment is made are, in feminist and anti-oppressive services, frameworks feminist social work built. The question is what happens to those frameworks inside a configuration organized by forced intimacy. Our answer is that some of them are absorbed by it, and that naming which ones is the condition of their refusal."""])

OPS[84] = ('REPLACE', ["""Consider, by way of orientation, the trajectory of a disclosure that this article is meant to make analytically legible. A woman in her thirties with a moderate intellectual disability has lived for fourteen years in a Belgian residential service. Daily care includes assistance with showering, dressing, transfers, and menstrual management, provided on rotating shifts by a small team of *aides-soignant·es* and *auxiliaires de vie*. Over a period of months, the contact of one of them — a man, in a workforce overwhelmingly composed of women, a disproportion that shapes how his conduct is read and how hers is — begins to exceed what those tasks require. The woman tells another resident, then an *éducateur* in her day programme, that he “touches her wrongly.” The protocol routes the disclosure to her *référent*, the social worker responsible for her file, who knows the care worker professionally and has previously attributed her statements about interpersonal events to confusion or to the volatility associated with her diagnosis. The *référent* re-reads the disclosure through that frame: as a possible misperception of necessary care, as a conflict requiring mediation, as a candidate for behavioural monitoring rather than safeguarding investigation. The care worker remains in post. The woman, asked to clarify her account in successive interviews structured by professional vocabularies she does not share, becomes less consistent and more distressed; this is recorded as further evidence of unreliability. The case is closed.""",
"""This trajectory is composite rather than singular, and its features are documented across the literature on institutional sexual violence (Hollomotz, 2011; Sobsey, 1994; Thiara et al., 2012) and in publicly available inspection and audit reports from Belgian and French regulators (Courteau et al., 2019). It is not exceptional. It is the routine product of the three systems working in concert: the normative condition of forced intimacy that makes the care worker's excess legible as continuous with legitimate care; the epistemic system that distributes credibility toward professional judgment; the professional system that absorbs the disclosure into institutional self-management.""",
"""The occupational geography of that trajectory determines where this article's argument bites. Four positions are involved and they are not interchangeable. The *aide-soignant* or *auxiliaire de vie* performs the intimate bodily work in which forced intimacy consists — the least protected and least remunerated position in the chain. The *éducateur·rice spécialisé·e* holds the daily relational mandate and is frequently the first recipient of a disclosure. The *référent* or case manager, typically an *assistant de service social*, holds the power of qualification: what the disclosure is, whether it is one, what happens next. Medical authority sits above all three and can requalify their judgments as clinical matters. Social work's characteristic position is thus not the site of intimate contact but the site of its interpretation, which is why an analysis of epistemic power is the form this critique has to take. It is not that social workers commit the violence described here. It is that the point at which the configuration converts a disclosure into a non-event is, institutionally, a social work decision."""])

OPS[79] = ('REPLACE', ["""The institutional arrangements that disabled people navigate share the defining features Freyd identifies as conditions for institutional betrayal: they are environments of dependency and trust, with strong incentives to protect their own reputation, that routinely position the accounts of disabled residents as less authoritative than the professional judgments of staff. In the continental medico-social context these dynamics are shaped by the tradition of specialized residential institutions and the comparative weakness of independent oversight (Revillard, 2020; Ville et al., 2020).""",
"""Three mechanisms of absorption can be distinguished, each documented in published scholarship and in publicly available inspection material, and each recurring in our own fieldwork across French and Belgian reporting systems (Authors, under review). The first is reattribution to impairment: possible signs of violation are absorbed into the explanatory reservoir of the disability itself — a bruise reattributed to known self-injurious movements, a change in behaviour to a syndrome or a medication — so that observations that would prompt concern in a non-disabled person are neutralized before they circulate (Courteau et al., 2019; Peña-Guzmán & Reynolds, 2019). The second is pre-emptive disqualification: the disabled person's account is discounted by invoking cognitive or communicative limitation before its content is examined, the mechanism Williams and Jobe (2024) trace through routine professional assessments of competence and consistency. The third is diffusion of responsibility: where concern surfaces, each professional assumes a colleague will report, the matter is noted in a file rather than escalated, and no formal *signalement* follows — the pattern Thiara and colleagues (2012) describe when social workers and care managers prioritized the continuation of care arrangements over protection from violence.""",
"""What these produce jointly is the inversion institutional betrayal names: the institution protects the continuity of the care relationship and its own standing before it protects the resident. That the pattern holds across two jurisdictions with different statutory reporting duties suggests it is a property of the configuration rather than of a particular legal regime."""])

OPS[88] = ('REPLACE', ["""Reeve's (2002, 2012, 2014) concept of psycho-emotional disablism names that relational dimension with precision. Drawing on Foucault's analysis of normalization and Honneth's theory of recognition, Reeve argues that disablist oppression operates through interpersonal interaction and internalized self-perception: being spoken over, treated as less than fully competent, having one's judgments subordinated to those of professionals. Social workers, despite commitments to empowerment and anti-oppressive practice, routinely enact this through the ordinary conduct of professional relationships — communication that addresses carers rather than disabled people, assessment frameworks centering functional deficits rather than capacities — which is why Reeve (2012) argues recognition should be taught as a foundational relational competency. In the context of sexual violence the stakes are severe: a disabled person whose account is met with doubt, or redirected toward care needs, encounters in the social work relationship a replication of the epistemic silencing analyzed above. This is how the *référent*'s decision in our composite becomes possible without anyone in the chain intending harm."""])

OPS[102] = ('REPLACE', ["""We have argued that sexual violence against disabled people in the continental medico-social regime is produced by a configuration in which mandated intimate access, professionally held interpretive authority, and institutional self-protection operate together, producing the exposure and its unsayability in a single movement. Two questions follow: how far does the analysis reach beyond the regime in which we developed it, and what does it ask of feminist social work? We take them in that order, because the answer to the first constrains the answer to the second.""",
"""The scope of the argument: mandated care beyond the medico-social. We specified the mechanism in Belgian and French medico-social institutions because that is where it is densest, best documented, and least theorized, and because the institutional particulars matter. But the specification is not a boundary. What produces the conjunction is not the continental regime as such; it is a set of conditions that regime instantiates with unusual clarity. Four are doing the work. Access to a person's body or life is institutionally mandated rather than chosen. That access is performed under a vocabulary of care, help, or treatment. The authority to interpret what happens within it is held professionally rather than by the person who undergoes it. And the institution that would receive a complaint is also the institution that arranges the access.""",
"""Wherever those conditions hold, the same conjunction should be expected, and the same intervention — improving the reception of disclosures without altering the arrangement — should be expected to fail in the same way. Deinstitutionalization in North America is an uneven and incomplete process rather than a completed transition, and the congregate settings that remain satisfy all four conditions (Ben-Moshe, 2020; Ben-Moshe et al., 2014; Rossiter & Rinaldi, 2018). More importantly, the conditions are not restricted to bodily intimacy or to disability. Where psychiatrized people live under community treatment orders, the mandated access is to compliance, to speech, and to the interior life: attendance is compulsory, disclosure to a worker is a condition of continued liberty, non-compliance carries the threat of re-institutionalization, and the professional holds the authority to interpret what compliance and insight consist in (Daley et al., 2019; LeFrançois et al., 2013). In child protection, mandated access to the home and to family intimacy is exercised under a helping vocabulary, and the same authority interprets what is seen and said. In each case the intimacy is relational or informational rather than physical, but the structure is the same, and so, we would predict, is the pre-disclosure foreclosure it produces.""",
"""We advance this as an analytic claim rather than an empirical finding: we have not studied those sites, and the forms violence takes in them are not identical to those we describe. What matters is that the analytic also specifies its own limits, which is what distinguishes it from a general appeal to structure. Where exit from the relationship is genuinely available, where independent advocacy is resourced and accessible, where the person's account holds standing that does not depend on professional ratification, or where the body receiving a complaint is not the body arranging the access, the conjunction weakens — and the mechanism predicts exactly that. This is why the accountability measures discussed below are not peripheral to the argument: they are the points at which one of the four conditions can be removed."""])

OPS[106] = ('REPLACE', ["""The second implication concerns disclosure practice. Feminist social work has developed extensive knowledge about trauma-informed, survivor-centered practice in gender-based violence (Fook, 2002; Lonsway & Archambault, 2012), and our argument is that such knowledge is necessary but insufficient here, because it addresses the reception of testimony while the mechanism we describe operates before testimony is given. Extending it requires three things. Practitioners need training to recognize and suspend the credibility hierarchies that position professional interpretation as more reliable than a disabled person's account, in the forms Williams and Jobe (2024) and Benedet and Grant (2007, 2014) document. Communication conditions need to be genuinely accessible rather than nominally accommodating — which means treating the interview format that made our composite's account less consistent as a safeguarding failure rather than an evidentiary finding. And disclosure has to be understood as a relational process shaped by the power dynamics of the professional relationship. Access intimacy is not a communication technique; it names a relationship in which a disabled person's epistemic authority over their own experience is affirmed before it is tested."""])

OPS[108] = ('REPLACE', ["""The third implication concerns institutional accountability, where the continental context is most specific and the leverage greatest, since these measures remove conditions rather than mitigate consequences. In Belgium and France, where large residential institutions remain the primary infrastructure of support and independent advocacy is comparatively underdeveloped (Revillard, 2020; Ville et al., 2020), the decisive point is that the body receiving a complaint is usually the body arranging the care. Instruments that could break that circuit already exist and are under-used rather than absent: in France, the *loi 2002-2* reforming social and medico-social action created a *charte des droits et libertés*, a *conseil de la vie sociale*, and a *personne qualifiée* whom users may call on to assert their rights; the *Défenseur des droits* offers an independent route for complaints of rights violations and discrimination; the *Contrôleur général des lieux de privation de liberté* monitors settings involving deprivation of liberty. Their limitation is uneven implementation, weak independence from providers, and limited accessibility to residents with intellectual or communication disabilities.""",
"""Three developments follow directly. Reporting routes must terminate outside the provider organization, since a route returning to the arranging institution reproduces the condition it is meant to remedy. Resident bodies need binding consultation rights over safeguarding policy rather than advisory status. And monitoring capacity needs to sit with disabled-led organizations rather than with providers or their funders, on the model of the survivor advocacy that followed the Huronia litigation in Canada and of the UK self-advocacy networks formed after Winterbourne View. We cite these as demonstrations that the absence of such mechanisms is contingent rather than necessary, not as templates for import: each was shaped by a specific legal and political context. The point for feminist social work in Belgium and France is that institutional accountability is available as a concrete object of advocacy, research, and practice."""])

OPS[114] = ('REPLACE', ["""A final implication concerns the question, explicit within critical disability studies and critical social work, of whether the response to institutional violence should be reformist or abolitionist — and our analysis does not leave it neutral. Abolitionist positions, articulated most forcefully in disability justice and Mad Studies and developed by scholars working across disability studies, criminology, and social work (Ben-Moshe, 2020; Ben-Moshe et al., 2014; Chapman & Withers, 2019), hold that institutions structured by mandated intimacy, dependency, and professional authority cannot be reformed in ways that durably remove the conditions of violation, and that the work is to dismantle large residential infrastructures in favour of community-based, disabled-led, interdependent support (Piepzna-Samarasinha, 2018; Sins Invalid, 2018). Reformist positions seek to make existing institutions less violent through training, safeguarding, advocacy, and survivor-centered practice. The four conditions we identified give the abolitionist argument a specific form: three can in principle be addressed by reform, but the first — that access is mandated rather than chosen — is constitutive of congregate provision itself. We do not resolve the debate. We note that the implications drawn here are reformist in scope, necessary insofar as disabled people live in these settings now and insufficient insofar as the abolitionist analysis of the first condition is correct, and that an analysis locating the mechanism in a mandated arrangement cannot consistently treat the future of that arrangement as someone else's question."""])

