OPS[8] = ('REPLACE', ["""Sexual violence against disabled people is well documented epidemiologically and forcefully denounced politically, yet it has rarely been theorized as a product of the ordinary organization of care. This conceptual article develops such a theorization from the medico-social systems of Belgium and France, where residential facilities remain the primary infrastructure of support for many disabled people and independent safeguarding is weak. Drawing on crip theory, feminist disability studies, epistemic injustice frameworks, and disability justice, we argue that forced intimacy (Mingus, 2017) is not one risk factor among others but the structural medium through which normative and epistemic systems are joined. Because intimate bodily access is mandated and routinized as care, it produces exposure to violation and, in the same movement, disables its recognition: epistemic injustice operates before disclosure, foreclosing the resources through which violation could be named, and not only after it, in the discounting of testimony already given. We trace how disclosures are subsequently absorbed — through reattribution of signs to impairment, pre-emptive disqualification of the disabled person's account, and diffusion of reporting responsibility — and argue that the analysis is portable beyond this regime, to other configurations in which relational or bodily access is professionally mandated and interpretive authority professionally held. Implications for feminist social work practice, education, and research are discussed."""])

OPS[13] = ('REPLACE', ["""Sexual violence against disabled people is, in the words of Mueller, Forber-Pratt, and Sriken (2019), a domain where disability remains “missing from the conversation.” Meta-analyses consistently document that disabled people face a significantly elevated risk of sexual violence compared to non-disabled people, with estimates ranging from 1.5 to 2.3 times higher and the risk rising sharply for people with psychosocial or intellectual disabilities (Hughes et al., 2012; Mailhot Amborski et al., 2022). Yet these figures circulate largely within epidemiological and legal literatures, disconnected from the frameworks feminist social work has developed to understand gender-based violence, and equally disconnected from the critical disability scholarship that has reframed the politics of disabled life."""])

OPS[67] = ('REPLACE', ["""If the first system is normative, organized around compulsory able-bodiedness and forced intimacy, the second is epistemic. Disabled people who report sexual violence do not simply face the general disbelief feminist scholars have documented as a structural feature of sexual assault disclosure (Kelly, 1988; Lonsway & Archambault, 2012). They face a compounded credibility deficit rooted in the intersection of gender, disability, and institutional position — a deficit that operates through mechanisms Fricker's (2007) framework allows us to name precisely, and that is reinforced by institutional betrayal (Smith & Freyd, 2013, 2014)."""])

# 61: fold the closing paragraph into the third mechanism to remove restatement
OPS[61] = ('REPLACE', ["""It is Mingus's (2017) concept of forced intimacy that most directly names the structural link between compulsory able-bodiedness and the conditions of sexual violence. Forced intimacy designates the systematic requirement, built into the daily lives of many disabled people, to surrender bodily privacy and intimate access to strangers and institutional actors in order to meet basic needs: to be bathed, dressed, transferred, fed, medicated. This is not incidental to disabled life as currently organized; it is a structural feature of institutional and semi-institutional care, particularly marked in the medico-social systems of continental Europe, where residential and day-care facilities remain the primary infrastructure of support (Revillard, 2020). Mingus is explicit that forced intimacy is not the same as sexual violence, and equally explicit that it creates the conditions in which sexual violation becomes possible, legible as routine rather than transgressive, and difficult to name or resist.""",
"""That last claim is the one this article takes up, because the literature has named the relation more often than it has specified it. If forced intimacy is not itself sexual violence, by what mechanisms does it produce the conditions of violation? Three operate together, and each has an empirical anchor in existing scholarship even where it has not been theorized in these terms.""",
"""The first is the desensitization of bodily boundary. When intimate physical contact with non-related others is a routine and unrenounceable feature of daily life, the perceptual and somatic markers that ordinarily signal violation — the unfamiliar hand, the unsanctioned proximity, the gesture that exceeds the task — lose their differentiating force. What would register as transgressive in another life is integrated, by repetition and by necessity, into the texture of care. This is the dynamic Sobsey (1994) identified when he showed that the compliance and deference systematically instilled in institutional settings disable precisely the self-protective responses that recognizing violation requires, and that Hollomotz (2011) documents in the accounts of adults with learning difficulties for whom the question of what may be refused had never been posed.""",
"""The second is interpretive blurring. The institutional and professional vocabularies that frame intimate contact as “care” supply ready descriptions for acts that fall outside it. A care worker who exceeds what hygiene or transfer requires can locate the action within the same lexical and gestural field as legitimate care, and so can the institutional culture around them; what becomes difficult is not the action but its renaming as violation. Thiara and colleagues (2012) document the consequence at the level of professional response, where disclosures by disabled women were consistently metabolized as questions about the adequacy of care arrangements rather than as reports of violence.""",
"""The third is the asymmetry of interpretive authority. In care relationships organized by forced intimacy, the worker holds substantial authority to define what is happening — what counts as accidental, as necessary, as proportionate — while the disabled person's interpretation is filtered through institutional doubts about cognitive, communicative, or emotional reliability. Benedet and Grant (2007, 2014) show how thoroughly this asymmetry is embedded even in legal settings designed to adjudicate it, and Williams and Jobe (2024) show it operating in the routine judgments through which police and social services assess disabled complainants. The relation between forced intimacy and sexual violence is thus neither identity nor analogy: forced intimacy is the structural medium in which the perceptual, lexical, and authority conditions for violation are assembled at once. This is why the arrangement cannot be addressed by improving the response to disclosure alone, and why the next section takes the epistemic asymmetry as its own object."""])

